34,2,,status,George,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,George,,"I've looked into the diary and external examples. It's clear that usage varies wildly by day, and other groups found that constantly asking for favors or clinging to turns is what truly sours the relationship.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings confirm that the administrative side is a major issue. Attempting to verify figures created significant tension and resentment, suggesting that a high-maintenance accounting system is counterproductive to our harmony.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are concerning. The car is often handed over with rubbish inside or nearly out of fuel. Furthermore, the mechanic noted that our frequent short trips are causing faster wear on the brakes and battery.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The financial data is quite revealing. The fixed overhead is over 280,000 yen, and fuel contributions are heavily lopsided, with some of us paying far more than others. We cannot ignore this imbalance in our new system.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found that the fuel receipts are very uneven, with some people paying much more than others. Also, recording every single run took a lot of time. I want us to be fair, but not too stressed.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,George,,It seems we are all feeling the same strain. @Toshio and I both see that the social friction—whether from administrative checking or asking for favors—is as damaging as the financial imbalance @Misaki and @Minato mentioned.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Misaki"",""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"Exactly. The mental energy spent on policing each other is a hidden cost. If we implement a system that requires constant verification, we are simply replacing one form of friction with another. We need a low-maintenance solution.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"I agree that we need a low-maintenance system, but it must include basic standards. If we ignore the car's condition and fuel levels at handover, the system will fail regardless of how we handle the money.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"While I understand the desire for low maintenance, we can't simply overlook the fact that some are paying significantly more than others. A system that ignores these disparities is not low-maintenance; it's just unfair.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minato,,@Misaki is right that it's not fair if some people pay more. But @Toshio is also right that fighting over numbers is sad. Can we find a way to be honest without it being a chore?,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,George,,"We must be careful not to let the pursuit of perfect fairness destroy our peace. If we create a system that requires constant policing, we are just inviting more of the tension we're trying to escape.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@George is correct. The data shows that checking figures leads to apologies and resentment. We should prioritize a system that minimizes the need for auditing, even if it means accepting a small margin of error for the sake of efficiency.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"Efficiency is important, but not at the cost of basic reliability. If we accept a ""margin of error"" that includes leaving the car empty or dirty, it creates a different kind of stress for the next driver.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,A margin of error in fuel costs is not a small detail when the imbalance is this significant. We need a system where costs are tied directly to individual usage to prevent anyone from quietly subsidizing others.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I feel a bit worried when we talk about ""margins of error"" if it means someone is being unfair. But I also don't want us to be grumpy with each other. Maybe there is a middle way?","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,George,,"The middle way is to focus on autonomy. If we establish clear, pre-set rules for fuel and cleanliness, we remove the need to ask favors or police each other, which is where the real bitterness begins.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. By automating the rules and reducing the need for manual verification, we can eliminate the administrative burden. The goal should be a system that runs itself, requiring minimal mental energy from any of us.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I can agree to that, provided the ""pre-set rules"" are non-negotiable. We need a guarantee that the car is clean and fueled for the next person, especially for those of us relying on it for morning commutes.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I can agree to non-negotiable rules for the car's condition, but the same must apply to the money. We need a clear mechanism to ensure costs are split based on actual use, not just a vague agreement.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I think we are all starting to agree. If we have clear rules for the car and a fair way to handle the money, we can all be happy and peaceful together.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
