58,3,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,George,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,George,,"Looking at these options, Option B seems the most promising. By having fixed days, we eliminate the need to ask favors or negotiate turns, which we know is what often sours these arrangements.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"I agree with George. Option B is the most efficient. It removes the need for constant coordination and the tedious auditing of figures, which we've already seen causes resentment. It's the only low-maintenance choice.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Option B is the only one that guarantees I can rely on the car for my morning needs. If I have my own days, I can ensure it's ready and available without any uncertainty.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Misaki,,I cannot support Option B. A fixed monthly share ignores the actual usage and costs we discovered. It would simply formalize a system where those who drive less quietly subsidize those who drive more.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minato,,"I understand why @Misaki is worried. If we just pay the same amount, it might not be fair to people who don't drive much. But Option A sounds like it takes a lot of work. Which one is the kindest?","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,George,,"@Minato, kindness comes from peace of mind. Option A requires constant recording and calculating, which we've seen leads to friction. Option B is the kindest because it gives everyone autonomy and removes the need to police one another.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Minato, the ""kindest"" system is the one that doesn't make us act like accountants. Option A is just a return to the same administrative nightmare we've been discussing. Option B is the only way to truly stop the stress.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Misaki, while I understand your concern about costs, the stress of an unreliable car is a cost too. Option B provides the stability I need. I would rather pay a fixed share than worry if the car is available.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Victoria, stability is important, but not at the expense of fairness. Option A is the only one that ensures costs are tied to actual use. I believe the effort of recording is worth avoiding an unfair financial burden.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Misaki, I want it to be fair too. But @Toshio and @George are right that recording everything is very tiring. Is there a way to be fair without making everyone feel stressed?","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Toshio"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,George,,"Perhaps we can accept that perfect financial fairness is less valuable than our friendship. Option B removes the ""favor-asking"" and the ""policing"" that destroy groups. Let's prioritize our peace of mind over a few yen.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,Exactly. The cost of our time and mental health is far higher than any minor discrepancy in fuel costs. Option B is the only choice that eliminates the administrative burden entirely. Let's choose efficiency over accounting.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. The mental toll of uncertainty and the frustration of finding the car in a poor state are far more draining than a fixed payment. Option B provides the reliability and peace of mind I need.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"I cannot agree that this is a ""minor discrepancy."" We are talking about hundreds of thousands of yen and a heavily lopsided fuel burden. True peace comes from a system that is actually fair, not one that just hides the unfairness.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Minato,,"I feel sad that we are still arguing. @Misaki, I really want it to be fair, but @Toshio and @George are right that the recording was very hard. Maybe Option B is okay if we are all honest?","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Toshio"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,George,,"Honesty is a start, but a system that doesn't rely on it is safer. Option B protects us from the friction of policing each other. For the sake of our harmony, I vote for Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"The evidence is clear: auditing and coordination are the primary sources of our conflict. To preserve our time and mental energy, we must choose the most streamlined path. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Victoria,,I value the reliability and stability that Option B provides. It ensures the car is available when I need it and removes the stress of uncertainty. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I cannot in good conscience vote for a system that ignores the financial reality we've uncovered. Fairness is the only sustainable foundation for our arrangement. To avoid unfair subsidies, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Minato,,"I want everyone to be happy and not fight. Option A is fair, but it's so tiring. Option B is peaceful, but @Misaki is right about the money. I'll vote for Option B for our peace.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
